WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:13.429
Hello! My name is Jo Bauer, but readers of Little Women may remember me as Jo March.

2
00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:20.517
Yes, I married Professor Fritz Bauer, and we operate a school called Plumfield.

3
00:00:20.518 --> 00:00:27.328
It's a few miles outside the town where I lived with my sisters, Meg, Beth, and Amy.

4
00:00:27.329 --> 00:00:33.886
Poor Beth passed away, but my other sisters are married and leading happy lives.

5
00:00:33.887 --> 00:00:39.358
My younger sister, Amy, is an artist and married to Theodore Laurence.

6
00:00:39.359 --> 00:00:43.551
We call him Laurie because we've known him for years.

7
00:00:43.552 --> 00:00:51.576
When we were growing up, he lived next door to us, and his tutor, John Brookes, married my older sister.

8
00:00:51.577 --> 00:00:55.057
Meg and John have ten-year-old twins.

9
00:00:55.058 --> 00:01:02.441
Demi is a Plumfield student, and Daisy is staying with us because Meg recently had another baby.

10
00:01:02.442 --> 00:01:13.150
But that's enough about the past. It's time to meet the Little Men of Plumfield—and Daisy too!

11
00:01:13.151 --> 00:01:22.166
*** One rainy spring Saturday, a ragged 12-year-old boy arrived at the door of a large, comfortable

12
00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:27.216
house. He gave a timid rap with the brass

13
00:01:27.217 --> 00:01:33.341
knocker. Although he could hear children playing inside, no one came to the

14
00:01:33.342 --> 00:01:39.780
door. He knocked again, but when no one answered, he hesitantly pushed open the

15
00:01:39.781 --> 00:01:42.841
door. And what a sight greeted

16
00:01:42.842 --> 00:01:52.410
him! Two boys about his age were playing tag while two younger boys chased a yellow puppy and a white

17
00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:58.419
one. Another boy sat quietly reading on the stairs.

18
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:06.614
"Oh, hello!" A dark-haired girl looked up from her doll. "Are you a new boy?"

19
00:02:06.615 --> 00:02:11.074
"I don't know yet," he said shyly.

20
00:02:11.075 --> 00:02:16.952
"My name's Nat Blake, and I have a letter for Mrs. Bauer from Mr. Theodore Laurence.

21
00:02:16.953 --> 00:02:19.885
He said she'd take care of me."

22
00:02:19.886 --> 00:02:24.421
"Wait here, and I'll take Uncle Laurie's letter to Aunt Jo.

23
00:02:24.422 --> 00:02:28.061
I'm Daisy, and that's my brother, Demi."

24
00:02:28.062 --> 00:02:34.600
Daisy pointed to the boy reading on the stairs, oblivious to what was happening around him.

25
00:02:34.601 --> 00:02:38.920
Suddenly one of the tag players slid down the banister.

26
00:02:38.921 --> 00:02:46.461
Unable to stop, the boy landed with a crash that would have knocked anyone else unconscious.

27
00:02:46.462 --> 00:02:53.289
Nat forgot his shyness and ran over, expecting to find the boy half-dead.

28
00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:59.651
However, the boy simply blinked rapidly and looked up at Nat.

29
00:02:59.652 --> 00:03:04.460
"Have you come to join us? I'm Tommy Bangs."

30
00:03:04.461 --> 00:03:08.682
Tommy jumped up, as if he'd just remembered his manners.

31
00:03:08.683 --> 00:03:13.410
"Come play tag with Jack Ford and me!"

32
00:03:13.411 --> 00:03:20.113
"I should probably wait to see whether I'm staying," Nat mumbled, looking around.

33
00:03:20.114 --> 00:03:25.298
He saw a large schoolroom and a cozy parlor.

34
00:03:25.299 --> 00:03:29.366
A dining room had a table set for supper.

35
00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:35.889
As the smell of gingerbread filled the air, Nat's desire to stay grew.

36
00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:39.968
Daisy returned with the news that Nat was to stay.

37
00:03:39.969 --> 00:03:42.796
She led him to Jo's study.

38
00:03:42.797 --> 00:03:45.749
"I'm delighted to see you!"

39
00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.889
Jo smoothed the hair from Nat's forehead.

40
00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:56.729
"I hope you'll be happy here. Oh my, but your feet are wet!"

41
00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:02.075
Jo bustled around and found Nat some slippers.

42
00:04:02.076 --> 00:04:04.160
She smiled at him.

43
00:04:04.161 --> 00:04:09.081
"These are too big, but that'll keep you from running away."

44
00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:17.268
"I don't want to run away, ma'am—" Nat dissolved in a fit of coughing.

45
00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:22.650
"How long have you been sick?" Jo asked, looking concerned.

46
00:04:22.651 --> 00:04:28.358
"I caught a cold this winter and never recovered," Nat said when he could speak again.

47
00:04:28.359 --> 00:04:33.299
"Sit by the fire, and we'll get some medicine into you."

48
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:39.192
Nat had just swallowed a spoonful of medicine when the bell rang for supper.

49
00:04:39.193 --> 00:04:45.940
Jo led him into the dining room, where he found a cheerful man sitting with six children.

50
00:04:45.941 --> 00:04:50.768
"Nat, this is my husband, Professor Bauer," Jo said.

51
00:04:50.769 --> 00:04:56.102
"And I think you've already met our two sons, Rob and Teddy.

52
00:04:56.103 --> 00:05:00.415
This is Jack, Tommy, Demi, and Daisy."

53
00:05:00.416 --> 00:05:07.468
Nat nodded shyly and took the empty seat next to Tommy.

54
00:05:07.469 --> 00:05:10.997
"Where did you used to go to school?" Tommy asked.

55
00:05:10.998 --> 00:05:16.717
"I didn't," Nat said, and Tommy's eyes widened in surprise.

56
00:05:16.718 --> 00:05:22.745
"I worked on the city streets, playing the fiddle with my father and a man named Nicolo."

57
00:05:22.746 --> 00:05:26.263
"That sounds like fun!" Tommy said.

58
00:05:26.264 --> 00:05:30.092
"It wasn't—I never had enough to eat."

59
00:05:30.093 --> 00:05:33.633
Nat paused to take a bite of gingerbread.

60
00:05:33.634 --> 00:05:39.098
"After my father died and I got sick, Nicolo took away my fiddle." "I

61
00:05:39.099 --> 00:05:46.863
bet Mr. Bauer will let you play his old fiddle," Tommy said, making Nat's face brighten with pleasure. Sure

62
00:05:46.864 --> 00:05:54.977
enough, after supper Jo handed a violin to Nat, and he began playing softly. As

63
00:05:54.978 --> 00:05:59.736
his confidence grew, the other children stepped closer. "Hey,

64
00:05:59.737 --> 00:06:03.590
you're good!" Tommy said. "Play

65
00:06:03.591 --> 00:06:09.836
something we know, and we'll sing along." Soon

66
00:06:09.837 --> 00:06:19.541
everyone was singing, but as the last notes died away, Nat began to sob. "Nat,

67
00:06:19.542 --> 00:06:24.225
what is it?" Jo asked with alarm. "You're

68
00:06:24.226 --> 00:06:32.479
all so kind, and—" Nat coughed until he was breathless. Jo

69
00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.613
wrapped her arms around him. "You

70
00:06:35.614 --> 00:06:40.612
have a father and mother now, and this is your home. Don't

71
00:06:40.613 --> 00:06:43.825
think of those sad times anymore." Nat

72
00:06:43.826 --> 00:06:48.780
gulped and managed a smile. ***

73
00:06:48.781 --> 00:06:56.118
After a bath, Nat relaxed in front of the fire while Jo bathed Teddy and Rob.

74
00:06:56.119 --> 00:07:02.410
When a pillow fight broke out, Jo laughed.

75
00:07:02.411 --> 00:07:06.221
"Aren't you going to punish those boys?" Nat asked.

76
00:07:06.222 --> 00:07:12.508
"Of course not," Jo said as Tommy ran into the room and tossed a pillow at her.

77
00:07:12.509 --> 00:07:15.622
She fired it right back at him.

78
00:07:15.623 --> 00:07:21.652
"Our boys do whatever they want on Saturdays, and that includes pillow fights."

79
00:07:21.653 --> 00:07:26.242
"What a nice school this is!" Nat sighed.

80
00:07:26.243 --> 00:07:40.026
"It's an odd one," Jo admitted, "but we don't believe in making children miserable with too many rules and too much studying."

